<BaseLayout title="Account-management portal — Dana" description="Research and product design for a clearer B2B account-management portal at Dialogue.">
  <header class="case-hero"><div class="container"><a class="back" href="/#work">← Selected work</a><p class="eyebrow">Dialogue · B2B product discovery</p><h1>Designing a clearer account-management portal</h1><p class="lead">A research-led concept that reorganized company profiles, billing, subscriptions, and support tasks into a focused experience for account teams.</p></div></header>

  <section class="tldr" aria-label="Project summary"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">At a glance</p><ul class="tldr-list"><li><b>Problem</b><span>Account managers relied on the portal plus Slack bots and IT request templates to do routine work.</span></li><li><b>What I did</b><span>I interviewed account managers, mapped the tools around the portal, and proposed an information architecture that separates internal operations from customer self-service, then prototyped it in two stages.</span></li><li><b>Result</b><span>A research-backed concept direction at the discovery stage. It did not ship, so there are no launch metrics.</span></li></ul></div></section>

  <section class="showcase" aria-labelledby="chain-title"><div class="container"><div class="showcase-head"><p class="eyebrow">From research to concept</p><h2 id="chain-title">Three artifacts, one line of reasoning</h2><p>Interviews surfaced the needs, the ecosystem map showed where the portal fell short, and the information architecture proposed a new structure. Select any artifact to jump to it.</p></div><div class="shots"><figure class="shot"><a href="#research"><img src="/images/dialogue/interview-synthesis.webp" alt="Research synthesis board grouping account-manager interview findings" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>1 · Interview synthesis</b><span>What account managers told me.</span></figcaption></figure><figure class="shot"><a href="#ecosystem"><img src="/images/dialogue/external-tools.webp" alt="Map of Slack bots and IT-support templates used by account managers" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>2 · Ecosystem map</b><span>The tools around the portal.</span></figcaption></figure><figure class="shot"><a href="#architecture"><img src="/images/dialogue/information-architecture.webp" alt="Proposed information architecture for internal account managers and customer administrators" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>3 · Proposed structure</b><span>Internal operations apart from customer self-service.</span></figcaption></figure></div></div></section>

  <section class="snapshot"><div class="container grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Project snapshot</p><h2>Untangling account work spread across systems</h2><p>Dialogue’s portal served both internal account managers and customer-side administrators. I explored how to separate internal operational work and build a focused portal around what each group needed.</p></div><dl><div><dt>My role</dt><dd>Product Designer leading research and design with the Product Manager</dd></div><div><dt>Users</dt><dd>Internal account managers and designated administrators at client organizations</dd></div><div><dt>Focus</dt><dd>Company profiles, members, billing, subscriptions, and support workflows</dd></div><div><dt>Methods</dt><dd>User interviews, synthesis, workflow mapping, information architecture, and prototyping</dd></div><div><dt>Stage</dt><dd>Discovery and concept development</dd></div></dl></div></section>

  <section><div class="container narrow"><p class="eyebrow">The challenge</p><h2>Account work extended far beyond the portal</h2><p class="large">Account managers used the portal alongside Slack bots, support-request templates, and help from technical teams. The scattered toolset made routine tasks hard to find and hard to complete consistently.</p><div class="challenge"><strong>Design challenge</strong><p>How might we create a dedicated account-management experience that reflects real workflows while separating internal operations from customer self-service?</p></div></div></section>

  <section class="tinted" id="research"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Research synthesis</p><h2>Turning interview evidence into product requirements</h2><p>I grouped interview findings into common themes and turned them into must-have capabilities for a future tool.</p></div><figure class="artifact featured"><a href="/images/dialogue/interview-synthesis.webp" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/dialogue/interview-synthesis.webp" alt="Research synthesis board grouping account-manager interview findings and common themes" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>User-interview synthesis</b><span>Clustered observations surfaced recurring needs, current behaviours, and product requirements.</span></figcaption></figure><div class="insights"><article><span>01</span><h3>Account data needed a dependable home</h3><p>Company and member information had to be easier to inspect and update without switching tools.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h3>Support was part of the workflow</h3><p>Slack bots and IT requests were not edge cases; they represented tasks the portal did not adequately support.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h3>Internal and client needs were different</h3><p>A clearer boundary was needed between operational administration and customer self-service.</p></article></div></div></section>

  <section id="ecosystem"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Current-state ecosystem</p><h2>Mapping the work outside the product</h2><p>Before proposing screens, I documented the external tools and support channels account managers used to finish their jobs.</p></div><figure class="artifact"><a href="/images/dialogue/external-tools.webp" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/dialogue/external-tools.webp" alt="Map of Slack bots and IT-support templates used by Dialogue account managers" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>External tools and workarounds</b><span>Slack bots and support requests revealed gaps between the portal and the full account-management job.</span></figcaption></figure></div></section>

  <section class="tinted"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Existing product</p><h2>Understanding the portal before changing it</h2><p>I mapped the current interface and its connections across billing, organizations, member records, imports, and profile details.</p></div><figure class="artifact"><a href="/images/dialogue/current-product-map.webp" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/dialogue/current-product-map.webp" alt="Current-state map of the account-management portal and its connected screens" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>Current product map</b><span>The map established the existing content model, paths, and dependencies before redesign.</span></figcaption></figure></div></section>

  <section id="architecture"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Information architecture</p><h2>Organizing the future product around account work</h2><p>The proposed hierarchy separated customer-facing administration from internal operational tools while preserving the relationships among organizations, members, subscriptions, and billing.</p></div><figure class="artifact"><a href="/images/dialogue/information-architecture.webp" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/dialogue/information-architecture.webp" alt="Proposed information architecture for internal account managers and customer administrators" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>Proposed information architecture</b><span>Two related structures clarify ownership and expose the capabilities each audience needs.</span></figcaption></figure></div></section>

  <section class="tinted"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Design progression</p><h2>Moving from structure to task-level interfaces</h2><p>Low-fidelity wireframes tested the structure of organization lists, profiles, billing information, and editing workflows before visual refinement.</p></div><div class="wireframes"><figure class="artifact"><a href="/images/dialogue/low-fi-foundation.webp" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/dialogue/low-fi-foundation.webp" alt="Low-fidelity wireframes for account and organization management" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>Foundation</b><span>Early screens established page structure and primary actions.</span></figcaption></figure><figure class="artifact"><a href="/images/dialogue/low-fi-interactions.webp" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/dialogue/low-fi-interactions.webp" alt="Low-fidelity interaction states for editing account information" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>Interaction states</b><span>Editing and detail states explored how account information could be managed in context.</span></figcaption></figure></div></div></section>

  <section class="prototype"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Prototype evolution</p><h2>Two stages of concept development</h2><p>The first prototype made the information architecture tangible. After product-planning conversations with the Product Manager, a later concept expanded the feature direction.</p></div><div class="videos"><article><div class="video-frame"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/TTSwbwwkU3c" title="Initial Dialogue account-management prototype" loading="lazy" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe></div><h3>Initial high-fidelity concept</h3><p>Translating the proposed hierarchy into an interactive product direction.</p></article><article><div class="video-frame"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/PT2-wEyPX2A" title="Evolved Dialogue account-management prototype" loading="lazy" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe></div><h3>Evolved feature concept</h3><p>Extending the experience after product-manager review and feature brainstorming.</p></article></div></div></section>

  <section><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">Proposed product model</p><h2>A focused workspace for recurring account tasks</h2><div class="capabilities"><article><span>01</span><h3>Organization profiles</h3><p>View and maintain company-level information in one dependable location.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h3>Member administration</h3><p>Inspect, add, and update member records within the appropriate organization.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h3>Billing and subscriptions</h3><p>Bring financial and plan information into the same account context.</p></article><article><span>04</span><h3>Guided support</h3><p>Reduce reliance on scattered tools by supporting recurring operational needs directly.</p></article></div></div></section>

  <section class="outcome"><div class="container grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Outcome</p><h2>A clearer product direction grounded in real work</h2></div><div><p>The project produced a synthesis of account-manager needs, maps of the current ecosystem and portal, a proposed information architecture, low-fidelity workflows, and two prototype stages.</p><p>This was a discovery project, so there are no launch metrics. If it moved forward, the next step would be to test the proposed information architecture with account managers and measure task completion on recurring work such as updating a company profile or changing billing details.</p></div></div></section>

  <section class="dark"><div class="container grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Reflection</p><h2>The workaround is part of the user experience</h2></div><div><p>Mapping only the portal would have missed much of the real job. Slack bots, IT requests, and manual support showed where the product no longer matched users’ responsibilities.</p><p>The most valuable move wasn’t a screen. It was widening the research frame before deciding what belonged in the new portal.</p></div></div></section>

  <section><div class="container narrow"><p class="eyebrow">Selected work</p><h2>See the shipped work</h2><p class="large">Two Canada Life projects went from research to release in the group-benefits portal.</p><div class="button-row"><a class="button" href="/work/estimates/">Read: pre-treatment estimates</a><a class="button button--secondary" href="/work/medical-claims/">Read: medical claims</a></div></div></section>

